  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 520 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nA deep upper low is spinning from southern Alberta into \nsouthern Saskatchewan early this morning, with an attendant \nsurface trough extending from the surface low over central \nSaskatchewan through western North Dakota. A preceding lower-\namplitude shortwave is traveling along the North Dakota-\nManitoba border and has been responsible for thunderstorms \nproducing excessive rainfall, especially from around Bottineau \nto Rugby. Ground-truth rain amounts around 4 inches have been \nreported, and MRMS is estimating near 6 inches locally. As of 5\nAM CDT, the threat for torrential rainfall has exited our \nforecast area. Farther upstream, isolated showers and storms \nthat are likely being maintained by a plume of low level warm \nair advection are moving from northwest into north central \nNorth Dakota. These have mostly been benign and were beginning \nto rapidly diminish as of 5 AM CDT, but earlier in the night \nthere was a rogue 58 mph wind gust at an NDAWN station near \nRay. The magnitude of warm air advection is forecast to \ndecrease later this morning, which could allow this activity to\ncontinue to dissipate. A few CAMs hint at redevelopment of weak\nelevated convection in northwest North Dakota later this \nmorning, likely in response to height falls from the \nSaskatchewan low, but more recent CAMs have backed off on this \npotential.\n\nStill looking at a conditional threat for severe thunderstorms \nin central and eastern North Dakota along and ahead of the \neastward advancing surface trough this afternoon and evening, \nwith uncertainty rooted in whether there will be strong enough \nforcing to overcome CIN. The most recent suite of CAMs is more \nconfident that convective initiation will occur at some point \nand time, but it may not be until closer to early evening when \nthe frontal boundary reaches the James River Valley. The SPC \nhas introduced a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) to the eastern \nthird of the state, reaching as far west as the James River \nValley. A discrete storm mode is favored, but splitting \nsupercells/storm mergers could contribute to upscale growth \nand/or clustering of storms. CAPE in the 2000-3000 J/kg range \nand deep layer shear around 35-40 kts will be supportive of \nvery large hail to around 2 inches in diameter. The amount of \nshear and high forecast DCAPE also leaves our most likely \nmaximum wind gust forecast at 70 mph. Did elect to begin \nmessaging a possible tornado, which would most likely be \nlimited to the James River Valley in the early evening when and\nwhere the combination of stronger low level shear/SRH and \nweaker MLCIN is maximized. \n\nToday will be seasonably warm, with highs in the lower 80s to \nlower 90s. A much drier air mass and gusty west-northwest winds \nbehind the surface trough will create near-critical fire \nweather conditions this afternoon and early evening, especially \nfrom near Williston to Bowman. The Canadian low will spin into \nsouthern Manitoba on Tuesday before diving southeast toward the \nwestern Great Lakes. This will leave cooler temperatures and \nwindy conditions over the Northern Plains on Tuesday, with \nhighs in the mid 70s to lower 80s and northwest winds around 20 \nto 30 mph, gusting as high as 40 mph. RH may fall low enough in \nthe far southwest for another afternoon of near-critical fire \nweather conditions on Tuesday. There could also be some \ndiurnally-driven afternoon showers in the cyclonic flow regime \non Tuesday. The NBM limits this potential to the Turtle \nMountains, but some runs of the HRRR have brought isolated \nshowers as far south as the northern James River Valley. \n\nBroad surface high pressure is forecast over the region on \nWednesday as the upper level ridge re-amplifies over the \nRockies. Guidance then favors the ridge axis being shoved \neastward on Thursday as a deeper trough digs into the Pacific \nNorthwest. Shortwave energy ejecting from the base of that \ntrough brings shower and thunderstorm chances back to the \nregion as early as Thursday night, with near-daily low to \nmedium chances continuing through the weekend as a more \nprogressive synoptic pattern emerges. Temperatures are favored \nto remain near average overall, but could trend slightly above \naverage closer to the end of the week.",
